{"name":"Mulberry Stain","hex":"#c6babe","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mulberry","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several trees, of the genus Morus, having edible fruits.","The fruit of this tree."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dark purple color tinted with red."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mulberry"},{"term":"stain","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A discolored spot or area caused by spillage or other contact with certain fluids or substances.","A blemish on one's character or reputation."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To discolor, as by spilling or other contact with a fluid or substance.","To taint or tarnish someone's character or reputation"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stain"}],"entries":[{"title":"Morus (plant)","blocks":[{"text":"Morus is a genus of flowering plants in the family Moraceae, consisting of 17 species of deciduous trees commonly known as mulberries, growing wild and under cultivation in many warm-temperate world regions. Generally, the genus has 64 subordinate taxa; although the three of most common are referred to as white, red, and black, originating from the colour of their dormant buds, and not necessarily the fruit colour, with numerous cultivars and some taxa currently unchecked and awaiting taxonomic scrutiny. Morus alba is native to China, but is widely cultivated and often naturalised across Europe, Southern Africa, South America, and North America; it is also the species most preferred by the silkworm. It is regarded as an invasive species in Brazil, the United States and some states of Australia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Mulberries_on_tree_1.jpg/330px-Mulberries_on_tree_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morus_(plant)","wikidata":"Q44789"},{"title":"Stain","blocks":[{"text":"A stain is a discoloration that can be clearly distinguished from the surface, material, or medium it is found upon. They are caused by the chemical or physical interaction of two dissimilar materials. Accidental staining may make materials appear used, degraded or permanently unclean. Intentional staining is used in biochemical research, and for artistic effect, such as in wood staining, rust staining and stained glass."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Patternspage.jpg/330px-Patternspage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stain","wikidata":"Q2733470"},{"title":"Mulberry (color)","blocks":[{"text":"The colour mulberry is a representation of the colour of mulberry jam or pie, a darker colour than that of the fresh fruit.\nTerms describing interchangeable shades, with overlapping RGB ranges, include burgundy, claret and maroon."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/29/Morus_alba_fruits.jpg/330px-Morus_alba_fruits.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mulberry_(color)","wikidata":"Q218218"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/%28MHNT%29_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg/500px-%28MHNT%29_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/%28MHNT%29_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg/960px-%28MHNT%29_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/%28MHNT%29_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg/500px-%28MHNT%29_Morus_alba_-_immatures_inflorescences_and_flowers.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_co